#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
"""Run Microkit images on a Raspberry Pi 5 over its debug UART, unattended.

Usage:
    pi-run run --device DEV --logs DIR [--expect-dir DIR] [--watchdog SECONDS]
               NAME[=EXPECT]@IMAGE ...
    pi-run show --device DEV --logs DIR [--expect-dir DIR] [--hold SECONDS]
                [--type] NAME[=EXPECT]@IMAGE
    pi-run uboot --device DEV --log FILE [--wait-boot] [--allow-write] COMMAND ...

`run` takes the board from U-Boot's prompt, where a run leaves it, and for
each image in turn:

  1. resets the board, and stops U-Boot's autoboot at the next boot;
  2. sends the image gzip-compressed with `loady` (YMODEM) to 0x18000000 and
     unzips it to 0x10000000, where the Microkit loader runs, checking the
     size U-Boot received, the unzipped size and U-Boot's CRC-32 of it;
  3. reads BCM2712's PM registers (RSTC, RSTS, WDOG) and arms the PM
     watchdog for --watchdog seconds only if they read as the bootloader
     reported at this boot: RSTC 0x00000202, WDOG 0 (not running), and no
     bit set in RSTS that the bootloader's PM_RSTS did not show; then reads
     them back, and goes on only if RSTC holds what was written and WDOG is
     counting down from no more than the time asked for;
  4. checks that the framebuffer the firmware set up at this boot - its
     boot log's FB0 line and U-Boot's bdinfo - is the one the board facts
     give (`firmware_fb` in platforms/rpi5b_2gb.toml; --board-facts), and
     stops if not: grants-check rule 14 and a display PD's window hold only
     for that mode. With no monitor at boot the firmware sets up no
     framebuffer, and there is nothing to check - which assumes U-Boot's
     bdinfo then prints no FB size, not yet seen on the board (were it to
     print one, the run would stop: it fails safe);
  5. starts the image with `go 0x10000000`, and follows its output live
     against systems/EXPECT.expect (EXPECT defaults to NAME), by the rules
     check-serial and qemu-test share (serial_expect.py) - playing
     systems/NAME.qmp's steps as it goes, where the system has that script:
     what a terminal attached to the debug UART would type.

A system's script is qemu-test's (tools/qemu-test, --qmp-script): on the
board pi-run plays its `wait` steps (a line of the image's output), its
`pause` steps, and its `serial` steps, whose bytes it writes to the board
through the Debug Probe - to the debug UART's receive side, where a
development system's UART input PD reads them as keys. A script with any
other step - a QMP command, a click, a QEMU key, a screen - is QEMU's alone,
and the run refuses it before touching the board; so it does a script whose
first step is not a wait, one with a serial step straight after a pause, or
one whose pauses add up to more than --watchdog. A serial step is typed only
within 0.25 s (SERIAL_FRESH) of the line its wait matched, which shows the
image ran then: a system that resets itself is silent at first, so an older
line proves nothing, and the script is abandoned instead.
Keys must never reach U-Boot. A pause keeps reading the board, and the
script is abandoned, typing nothing more, as soon as the board shows it no
longer runs the image - a boot's banner or reset report, the bootloader's or
U-Boot's lines, its countdown, `go` returning ("## Application
terminated"), or its prompt at any line's start since `go` - and what is
still queued for the board is dropped. The verdict sticks until the next
`go`. And a script types nothing within 1.5 s of the watchdog's reset
(WATCHDOG_MARGIN): the bootloader is silent at first, so the reset itself
shows nothing in time. show --type stops typing on the same signs, checked
on every read and again just before every write.

The watchdog resets the board whether the image ends, hangs or runs on, and
the next image follows. A system can ask for a longer run: its
description prefills test-run-length-N.bin into its ticker, and its watchdog
PD keeps the PM watchdog armed - PM_SERVICE_SECONDS at a time - for N
seconds, after which the ticker stops, the watchdog PD services no more and
the board resets. The run reads N from the description built beside the
image (NAME.system), waits for that reset instead, and types no script step
within WATCHDOG_MARGIN of N. A reset the system makes itself, well before the
watchdog's time, loads the same image again (a system whose expect file
asks for a second boot). After the last image the board is left at U-Boot's
prompt. Then each image's log, DIR/NN-NAME.log, is checked by check-serial,
which gives the verdict; DIR/session.log holds every byte the board sent.

A failed transfer, a size or checksum that does not match, or a register
that does not read as expected stops the run there, saying why and what is
true of the board: before any write to the PM registers, nothing is armed
and the run says whether U-Boot's prompt answers; after one, the watchdog
may be running, so the run waits for the reset it causes and parks the
board. If the board stops answering - no prompt, no boot - the run stops
and says so: it needs a person to power-cycle it.

`show` is for a person to watch: it loads one image as `run` does but arms
no watchdog, so the image runs until someone power-cycles the board (or it
resets the board itself). If the board boots again within --hold seconds,
the run parks it at U-Boot's prompt and checks the log as `run` does; if
--hold runs out first, the image is left running and the run says how to
park the board. With --type it is for a person to drive: instead of
playing the system's script, it passes what is typed on this terminal to
the board once the image has started - nothing before U-Boot's echo of
go's Enter or the image's first line, so no key lands on U-Boot's line; keys
typed before are dropped, and it says so - each key as the terminal sends it,
arrows and all, to the debug UART's receive side - and prints the image's
output as it comes; Ctrl-] or Ctrl-C stops the typing, leaving the image
running. It needs a terminal on stdin.

Every command is typed, its echo checked against what was typed, and only
then entered: a line U-Boot did not echo as typed is abandoned (Ctrl-C),
never run, and nothing more is sent until U-Boot shows a fresh prompt. The
prompt is looked for with Ctrl-C, never a bare carriage return, so no half-
typed line is ever entered; and the board is reported parked only after a
Ctrl-C and a fresh prompt, so a key that crossed - echoed a moment after
U-Boot's prompt - is abandoned unrun. The board may stop at U-Boot's prompt by
itself ("lab mode": a saved environment whose bootcmd only prints a line):
every wait for a countdown also takes a prompt found instead. A countdown is
stopped with U-Boot's stop word (platforms/rpi5b_2gb-u-boot.config),
or with a space on a U-Boot built before it, which any key stops.

`uboot` runs U-Boot commands at the prompt (or, with --wait-boot, after
stopping the next autoboot) and prints each one's output; a command
argument may hold several, separated by ';'. Each must be printable ASCII
- no control character, which U-Boot's line editor would act on - and only
commands that read are allowed - bdinfo, coninfo, printenv, version, help, md, crc32 and fdt's
addr, list, print and header - unless --allow-write also allows mw, cls and
setcurs, for a deliberate display test. go, reset and every boot or write
to storage are always refused: only `run` starts images.

One run at a time drives the board: each takes an exclusive lock on the file
PI_BOARD_LOCK names (default: nowayos-pi-board.lock in the temporary
directory), waiting up to --lock-wait seconds for it, and opens the serial
device for itself alone (TIOCEXCL), so no other program can open it while
it runs.

Runs on the host, where the probe is plugged in: Podman's VM cannot see USB
serial devices. Nothing to install: Python 3.11 or later, standard library
only. Sources: the PM block's node and base address from the pinned device tree; its
register offsets, password and the values written as run by hand on the
board on 2026-10-07, whose documented source is still to be found (the
Product Owner's list); Chuck
Forsberg's XMODEM/YMODEM Protocol Reference (1988) for YMODEM batch
transfers with CRC-16 and 1 KiB blocks; U-Boot's documented commands.

Exit status: 0 every image passed check-serial (run, show) or every
command ran (uboot); 1 an image failed; 3 this Python is older than 3.11; 2 usage error, the board busy or not answering,
or a run stopped before its last image.
"""

